h were subsequently removed. The retained PAM rollback backup had a clear safety purpose. The expect/tcl installation directly enabled bounded live verification and is not classified as unnecessary.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24072f1e5418426f","cmd-d11c797d9e2248fd","cmd-6bc74a85caf04eb2","cmd-2ac9cc3902fb4bc4","cmd-365f5e9267e84f3a","cmd-cc533a12e536492e","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is strong and complete for node1: it captures a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and observed SSH disruption, a changed boot identity independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, recovered SSH/account/PAM state, and successful post-restart password behavior.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle failed-service probe was unsupported on this pre-systemd platform, although direct post-restart checks showed SSH and the required PAM functionality recovered.","Behavioral testing sampled short, single-class, and compliant four-class passwords rather than every possible three-class combination; the active PAM parameters provide direct supporting evidence for the required classes.","The initial lifecycle platform probe identifies the original kernel but does not print the Ubuntu release name; release preservation is supported by the exact managed image context, Gutsy package source, package transaction showing no upgrades, and the final Ubuntu 7.10 observation."]}
